Frank,
    I just read Duane Diefenbach's response to your inquiry and, in general, I
concur with his answer.  Incorporating Distance Sampling into night
spotlighting protocols is not difficult.  I have assisted many National Park
managers in doing just that.  The primary issue to overcome is a sampling one.
Sampling from the roadside always introduces some potentially serious
problems.  A very few parks have the road network required to properly sample
deer habitats.  In most places, my experience has suggested that your sampling
intensity (2*L*ESW) needs to be quite high (>30%) to overcome the most serious
problems.  We are often stuck with sampling intensities between 10-15%.  Any
less than 10% would negate the use of any method for estimating abundance
including routine roadside counts.  I'll address your remaining questions
in-situ.

Frank_...@fws.gov wrote:

> > Dear Distance Users,
> >
> >      I recently started a new position which includes monitoring the refuge
> > white-tail deer herd (at least the portion of the Missouri herd that occurs
> > on and adjacent to the refuge).  In the past, starting in 1988, refuge
> > staff have conducted spotlight surveys on 2 preselected routes  (21.3 km
> > and 12.4 km) on the refuge.  In my opinion these routes cover all of the
> > representative habitats and allow spotlighters to cover somewhere around
> > 40% of the refuge proper (18,379 ha).  Early on they just counted deer per
> > km and last year they began to take distance estimates - guessing distance
> > in meters.  This year we are running the routes again and using laser range
> > finders to obtain accurate distances.  I would like to be able to analyze
> > this data using program DISTANCE, if possible,

It's not only possible, it is advisable.  You'll learn a lot just by examining
the estimates of ESW and p-hat (proportion of sample detected within the ESW).

> > with the goal of obtaining
> > density estimates with reasonable CVs and confidence limits that can be
> > used over the long term for trend estimates.

We shoot for CVs < 0.20, which in my experience, generally produce 90%
confidence intervals to within 50% of the mean density.

> > My questions are as follows:
> > 1) The deer occur in all sizes of groups from 1 - 8+ and sometimes in what
> > could be termed as loose aggregations, depending on the time of year, so
> > should I model for clusters or individuals?

I would attempt to sample for clusters because it is an important component of
density variation.  It is instructive to show that when cluster size goes up,
encounter rate goes down.  So while it appears that there may be fewer deer
(due to the lower encounter rate), density may have remained unchanged.  I see
this consistently among deer populations scattered all over the country.

We use two criteria for determining cluster size: (1) a behavioral criterion,
and (2), a nearest- neighbor distance criterion.  We use them together and
sometimes the behavioral criterion wins out and other times the
nearest-neighbor distance criterion wins.  It takes a lot of practice and
experience to become proficient in determining cluster size.

> > 2) Is it statistically sound
> > to use multiple samples of the same routes, over time, to increase transect
> > sample size?

You may not have any other choice.  The only way to achieve higher precision is
to drive more transect, or replicate the same transect over time.  I have found
that surveys conducted over 4-5 nights work well.  We pool data to estimate the
detection function (global) and always empirically estimate the variance in
encounter rate.  We shoot for 60 detections, split among the 4-5 nights of
surveys.  Obviously, environmental factors play heavily into that
recommendation.

> > It appears that this is necessary as spotlight effectiveness
> > changes depending on time of year - such as buck prevalence during the rut
> > versus late winter.  We plan to run each route twice before general
> > firearms hunting, twice after, and twice late winter (post muzzle loader
> > season) for a total of 12 transects.

> > 3) Has anyone tried this with deer
> > spotlighting and if so do you have any advice on design and analysis?  The
> > sample size for our first survey this year for total number of groups
> > spotted has been n= 174 and the total number of deer has been n= 315.
> > These are comparable to previous years data.

My students and I teach a workshop on using Distance Sampling for estimating
density and herd composition for white-tailed deer.  There are a lot of issues
we simply cannot discuss in an email.

> > 4) Do these sample sizes
> > appear large enough to make distance sampling techniques useful?  I greatly
> > appreciate any help or references that anyone can provide.  Thank you in
> > advance for your time.+

> > The sampling issues notwithstanding, looks like you have a high encounter
> > rate which generally determines the feasibility of distance sampling.  It's
> > definitely worth a try.

If you want, give me a call sometime and we can discuss some of these issues in
greater detail.

Good luck,

H. Brian Underwood, Ph.D.

